Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string &markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::istringstream stream(markdown);
    html::Options options;
    html = html::to_html(stream, options);
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a sample markdown text.\n**This is bold text.**\n*This is italic text.*";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);

    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This function uses the `html` library to convert the markdown text to HTML. The `html` library provides a function called `to_html` that takes a string and some options object, and returns the corresponding HTML string.

To compile this function, you'll need to include the `html` library. You can do this by adding the following line to your program:

```cpp
#include <html.h>
```

Make sure to link the library with your compiler as well.

